p('<b>A conditional WRRA computation and a precise limit on address-color coupling</b><br/>Wonsik Choi and Jeongin Choi<br/>Independent Research, Seoul, Republic of Korea<br/>20 October 2026 | v0.7<br/>janefather@gmail.com | ORCID 0009-0001-4263-9772')
h('Abstract')
p('We audit the particle/component branch of Minimal Computing Cosmology 3.0 and implement a source-specific release gate that distinguishes verified replay from unsupported particle/color interpretations. Frozen-input replay reproduces 427,892 odd-composite addresses, phenotype weight 0.050000000000000065 and assembly-order total-variation distance 0.20472100116369085. The supplied antisymmetric three-color state is annihilated by all eight total generators. Exact singlet support in three fundamental factors forces address-color factorization, restricting what a color-only interface can retain. Source inspection and restricted-namespace execution establish that the original color routine reads no arithmetic state. These findings now drive an executable development decision: the delivered gate accepts conservation, declared color consistency and the conditional factorization claim, but rejects an arithmetic-origin claim and an order-independent-decoder claim even though the original 36 checks pass. The work is a bounded reproducibility and implementation note; it adds no new SU(3) representation theory, particle identification or observational validation.')
h('1. One development question and its actual use')
p('The question is whether a successful combined Stage-3 replay supports each proposed claim about its arithmetic component and color interfaces. Component statistics and color invariance are different outputs of the same package. The replay measures an order ambiguity relevant to the component decoder; dependency inspection tests whether the color output depends on that decoder; the kernel proof restricts what the declared color state can encode. Neither branch alone answers all three release questions.')
p('The users of the artifact are the authors and maintainers packaging this WRRA branch for review or extending its decoder. This revision adds claim_gate.py to that actual release workflow. It prevents the package-level word PASS from being transferred to claims not exercised by the successful tests. The audited original already calls its decoder conditional; we do not attribute an arithmetic-origin claim or a deployed decoder bug to it. The implemented change is the new release decision procedure, whose acceptance and rejection cases appear in Section 4.')
h('Assessment contract')
p('<b>Inputs:</b> frozen address calibration, inherited assembly/charge rules, adopted three-color representation, and explicitly declared release-claim rules.<br/><b>WRRA transformation:</b> weighted address assembly and color checks feed a dependency and information-retention audit, followed by claim eligibility decisions.<br/><b>Outputs:</b> reproduced component statistics, color residuals, exact factorization and executable accepted/rejected claim records.<br/><b>Falsification:</b> failed replay, a violated generator/kernel identity, a counterexample to factorization under the stated support assumptions, or gate behavior inconsistent with its declared predicates rejects the corresponding implementation claim. This gate does not decide scientific merit or observational validity.')
page()
h('2. Frozen component replay and the supplied color branch')
p('WRRA abbreviates Worldline-Residue-Resource-Action. The frozen upstream commit is 9d9daf21154e46e55b9fd0fd461c7c070156ab94. Active addresses are n=2,...,1,015,000, weighted as n^(-alpha), alpha=1.8996877935161325. The admission threshold h=1.4476744689338703 is retained without refitting. On odd composites, e_phi=1-product_k[1-sigmoid(d_k-h)] for k=0,...,7, where d_k=sum_j cos[gamma_j(log n+0.1k)]/2 and sigmoid(x)=1/(1+exp(-x)). Other addresses have zero phenotype effect. Adopted gamma values are 14.134725141734695, 21.022039638771556, 25.01085758014569 and 30.424876125859512.')
eq('p(n)=w(n)e_phi(n) / sum_m w(m)e_phi(m)')
p('For each pool of the first K prime labels, initialize floor(n/sum(primes)) copies of every prime, then traverse the chosen ascending or descending order, taking one prime when the residue permits, until it is below two. Integer reconstruction n=sum_j count_j prime_j+residue is preserved. The candidate fraction is the p-weighted count of historically selected labels {5,47}, divided by the p-weighted total component count. Particle names, those labels and the 16-channel charge table are supplied. The exact rational neutral motifs are uud+electron, udd, an electron/conjugate pair and an individually neutral channel.')
table([['Branch','Candidate fraction / mean component count'],*[[k,f"{v['neutron_fraction_5_47']:.12f} / {v['mean_parts']:.10f}"] for k,v in a['profiles'].items()]])
p('For aligned component-number distributions P_j, TV=(1/2)sum_j |P_j(small)-P_j(large)|=0.20472100116369085 at K=15. Thus the tested assembly is order dependent. Fixed chosen orders remain legitimate algorithms; an order-independent decoder is a stronger claim. No observed neutron abundance is inferred from these conditional component fractions.')
eq('|S>=(|rgb>+|gbr>+|brg>-|rbg>-|grb>-|bgr>)/sqrt(6)\nQ_a=T_a tensor I tensor I + I tensor T_a tensor I + I tensor I tensor T_a')
p('Standard fundamental generators satisfy Tr(T_a T_b)=delta_ab/2. The original color_check() uses twice this normalization; annihilation and the kernel are unchanged. It constructs the three-dimensional tensor, signed permutations and eight matrices internally, and reads no address or assembly data.')
